pySMESH: make StdMeshers_Adaptive1D keep its size bounds and the factor 2 on every EDGE
(SMESH V9_16_0; applies after StdMeshers_Adaptive1D_deflection.patch).

Defect: the Adaptive hypothesis documents that the segment length "is limited by Min. Size
and Max Size" and that adjacent segments "can't differ more than twice"
(doc/gui/input/1d_meshing_hypo.rst). The algorithm breaks both on EDGEs where the
deflection holds, so the deflection patch, which only acts where a segment misses the
deflection, leaves them broken.

Symptom (min 0.05, max 1, deflection as given): on a B-spline prism neighbouring segments
differ by up to 3.23 times (d 0.03) and a segment is 0.049888 long (d 0.003); a B-spline bump
up to 3.15, a B-spline arc up to 2.74, the golden cone 2.16 (its seam) and 2.06 (its radius-2
circle), an ellipse 2 x 1.5 at d 0.03 2.51. With min 0.2 and d 1e-4 the straight EDGEs of the
B-spline prism get segments of 0.194143.

Root cause: the grading lives only in the size field. SegSizeTree::SetSize gives the six
neighbouring octree cells at most size + boxSize * 0.7 (grading 0.7 in AdaptiveAlgo::Compute),
so a neighbour cell holds at most 1.7 times the size; the size is clamped below by the min
size only in the tree. makeSegments() then reads the field at the probe points and at the
tree leaves, interpolates it in parameter, places the nodes on the integral of 1 / size and
rounds the count with the factor fact. Nothing checks the final segments: a jump between two
probe points or two leaves gives neighbours that differ more than twice, and fact > 1 shrinks
segments that sit at the min size below it. The code is unchanged since the hypothesis was
added (SMESH 7b33bc39f, 2013-11-11, "22355: EDF SMESH: New 1D hypothesis Adaptive"; the
count rounding is from 831ca6c82, 2015-11-19); upstream master at 83f8bf482 (2026-09-14)
has the same code.

Change, in StdMeshers_Adaptive1D.cxx only, on top of the deflection patch:
* holdDeflection() becomes holdRules(), called by makeSegments() for every EDGE, straight
  EDGEs included (they meet the deflection, so only the size rules are checked on them).
* New AdaptiveAlgo::breaksBounds(): true if a segment is shorter than min size or longer
  than max size (relative tolerance 1e-9), or if two neighbours on the EDGE differ more than
  twice, the two ends of a closed EDGE included.
* holdRules(): if no segment misses the deflection and breaksBounds() is false, the
  parameters stay as they are, bit for bit. Otherwise the EDGE is placed again by the code of
  the deflection patch (graded field, slope 0.5, so neighbours differ by at most
  e^0.5 = 1.65; count kept within the bounds; field scaled within [floor, max size]; floor
  raised where a floor-sized arc gives a chord below min size). After each placement, the
  larger of two neighbours that differ more than twice gets its field lowered to
  0.9 x min( 2 x the smaller, its field ), as a segment that misses the deflection does.

Order of the rules when they conflict: min size and max size first; then the factor 2,
resolved by grading the larger neighbour down, which never makes a segment longer and so
never breaks the deflection or max size, and never goes below the smaller neighbour, so never
below min size; then the deflection, which gives way to min size. Where the field is at its
floor, min size wins and the larger neighbour is not lowered further. In the cases measured
for this patch no EDGE needed that exception. An EDGE shorter than min size (one segment), or
a closed EDGE shorter than three min sizes, cannot meet the bounds; it keeps the count the
bounds allow.

Scope of the factor 2: upstream grades the size in space (the octree is 3-D), so its intent
covers neighbours across a VERTEX and EDGEs that pass close to each other. This patch checks
and enforces the factor 2 only along each EDGE and across the two ends of a closed EDGE;
behaviour across VERTEXes is unchanged.

Not changed: an EDGE whose segments meet all three rules keeps the segments of
makeSegments(); the size tree, the other EDGEs and Evaluate() are untouched. If the rules are
still broken after 200 refinements, the EDGE fails with a compute error.

